Review this TypeScript function for edge cases.

```ts
export function chunk<T>(arr: T[], size: number): T[][] {
  const out: T[][] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += size) {
    out.push(arr.slice(i, i + size));
  }
  return out;
}
```

Findings:
1. Medium: `size <= 0` causes an infinite loop (`i += size`).
2. Low: no validation for non-integer `size`.

Fix:
```ts
export function chunk<T>(arr: T[], size: number): T[][] {
  if (!Number.isInteger(size) || size < 1) {
    throw new Error("size must be a positive integer");
  }
  const out: T[][] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += size) {
    out.push(arr.slice(i, i + size));
  }
  return out;
}
```

Tests:
- size=1 returns singletons
- size larger than length returns one chunk
- size=0 throws

```ts
export type ParseResult<T> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: string };

export function safeJsonParse<T = unknown>(input: string): ParseResult<T> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(input) as T };
  } catch (err) {
    return { ok: false, error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : "invalid JSON" };
  }
}
```

Tests:
- valid JSON object
- invalid JSON
- primitive JSON values

Review this TypeScript map utility.

```ts
export function toMap(items: {id: string}[]) {
  const m: Record<string, {id: string}> = {};
  items.forEach((x) => m[x.id] = x);
  return m;
}
```

Findings:
1. Low: duplicate IDs silently overwrite earlier entries.
2. Low: return type should be explicit for maintainability.

Improved version:
```ts
export function toMap(items: { id: string }[]): Record<string, { id: string }> {
  const out: Record<string, { id: string }> = {};
  for (const item of items) {
    out[item.id] = item;
  }
  return out;
}
```

Optional strict variant: throw on duplicate IDs if your domain requires uniqueness.
